use ../../../lib/UiComponent

export ui-component Avatar:
    description:
        An avatar is a small picture that stands for a person or thing, such as
        a user's profile photo. If there is no picture, or it can't be loaded,
        it shows something in its place, such as the person's initials or a
        generic icon.

    picture:
        The component gives it a picture to show. The fallback is shown while
        the picture loads, and the picture replaces it once it has loaded. If
        the picture fails to load, the fallback stays. The component using the
        avatar is told whether the picture loaded or failed.

    fallback:
        The component chooses the fallback, usually initials taken from a name,
        or an icon. Whether the fallback appears straight away or after a
        short wait, so it doesn't flash up on a fast connection, is up to the
        chosen library.

    changing:
        When the picture changes, the new one loads the same way, with the
        fallback shown until it is ready. An old picture is never left showing
        in place of a new one that failed.

    name:
        An avatar has a name that assistive tools read out, usually the
        person's name, whichever of the picture or fallback is showing. If
        the same name is already shown next to it, the avatar is hidden from
        assistive tools instead, so the name isn't read out twice.

    appearance:
        Its size and shape, such as round or square, are up to the chosen
        library. The picture fills the shape without being stretched.

    nonGoals:
        - Nothing happens when it is pressed. An avatar that opens a profile is
            placed inside a Button or Link
        - No status dot, such as "online". A badge on top of it is a separate
            component
        - No stacks of avatars with a "+3" count. That's a separate component
            that uses avatars
